  "id": "DrugBank_DB12214 - MONDO_5015",
  "verbalization": "Luseogliflozin, a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor, is indicated for the management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, as evidenced by its relationships with Voglibose, Nateglinide, and Vildagliptin, all of which are indicated for the same disease. The integration of these pathways suggests that Luseogliflozin may exert a therapeutic effect on diabetes through mechanisms that involve the SGLT2 transporter, which is involved in glucose reabsorption in the kidneys. The presence of multiple indications pointing to Diabetes Mellitus (disease) from Luseogliflozin supports a potential role in the pathophysiology of the disease, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence is supported by the clinical indication and the indirect pathways linking Luseogliflozin to the disease, which align with the expected pharmacological effects of SGLT2 inhibitors. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Luseogliflozin to be repurposed for the treatment of diabetes, based on its established role in the disease.",
